<section class="brief" aria-labelledby="inbrief">
  <h2 id="inbrief">In brief</h2>
  <p class="lead">We asked eleven open AI models, each small enough to run on one gaming graphics card, to find
  10,000 numbers in the SEC filings of 100 US companies across ten industries. Every answer had to quote the exact line
  it was read from, so a computer could check it. We wanted to know which numbers are hard, and why.</p>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="v">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (H.pooled[H.sec_best].acc - H.pooled[H.sec_worst].acc)) }} pts</div><div class="l">accuracy gap between the easiest and hardest sector, against {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (H.acc_top - H.acc_bottom)) }} points between the best and worst model</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="v bad">×{{ "%.1f" | format(hh.H2.or) }}</div><div class="l">odds of a quietly wrong answer when the unit ("in millions") is printed away from the number</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="v">{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.og_digits) }}%</div><div class="l">of quietly wrong answers on numbers with no unit on the page had the right digits at the wrong scale</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="v">{{ H.c3_lower | length }} of {{ H.c3_models | length }}</div><div class="l">models made significantly fewer quietly wrong answers when allowed to think; retrying added them for {{ H.c2_raises | length }} of 10</div></div>
  </div>
  <ol class="findings">
    <li><b>The industry hardly matters; the model matters most.</b> Pooled over models, accuracy runs from {{ H.pooled[H.sec_worst].acc | pct }} ({{ H.sec_worst | short | lower }}) to {{ H.pooled[H.sec_best].acc | pct }} ({{ H.sec_best | short | lower }}), and the sector effect is not significant once companies are taken into account. Models run from {{ H.acc_bottom | pct }} to {{ H.acc_top | pct }}.</li>
    <li><b>How a number is printed drives the errors.</b> Units stated away from the figure, more figures on the line, the answer sitting in a later column, and year-to-date periods each make a number significantly harder, with the other factors held fixed, and each keeps its direction under a second prompt and a second table layout.</li>
    <li><b>The one sector that looked dangerous wasn't, once we looked closely.</b> Oil and gas had the most quietly wrong answers ({{ H.og.cw_all | pct }}), but almost entirely because {{ H.og.share_unitless | pct0 }} of its numbers have no unit anywhere on the page. Without those, it is ordinary ({{ H.og.cw_without_unitless | pct }}).</li>
    <li><b>v1's main finding holds on ten times the data.</b> Thinking cuts quietly wrong answers ({{ H.pooled_c3.b10 }} fixed, {{ H.pooled_c3.b01 }} introduced); telling a model its quote failed and letting it retry raises accuracy but adds quietly wrong answers.</li>
    <li><b>A quote check must survive formatting.</b> When tables were shown as Markdown, models made fewer column errors, but many dropped the table pipes when quoting, so a strict check rejected right answers. Any evidence checker needs to tolerate layout.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

