<article class="body">

<h2 id="abstract">Abstract</h2>
<div class="abstract">
<p>Small open language models can extract numbers from SEC filings with evidence a machine can check, but they still
make errors that pass the check. We ask which numbers cause those errors. In a pre-registered follow-up to
Filing-Evidence, we built {{ d.n_questions | num }} questions over {{ d.n_filings }} recent 10-K and 10-Q filings of
{{ d.n_companies }} US companies in ten sectors, with gold answers from the filers' inline XBRL and difficulty tags on
every question, fixed before any model ran. Eleven instruct models (7–21B parameters) answered on one RTX 4090 in
three setups: plain, check-and-retry, and thinking. Sector explains little: pooled accuracy ranges from
{{ H.pooled[H.sec_worst].acc | pct }} to {{ H.pooled[H.sec_best].acc | pct }} across sectors, against
{{ H.acc_bottom | pct }} to {{ H.acc_top | pct }} across models, and the sector term is not significant
(p = {{ d.h1.p | p }}). Presentation explains much more: a unit stated away from the figure raises the odds of a
wrong-but-passing answer {{ "%.1f" | format(hh.H2.or) }}-fold; each additional figure on the line, a later column and a
year-to-date period each lower the odds of a correct answer (odds ratios {{ "%.2f" | format(hh.H3.or) }},
{{ "%.2f" | format(hh.H4.or) }}, {{ "%.2f" | format(hh.H5.or) }}). The worst sector for wrong-but-passing answers,
oil and gas, is explained by numbers whose unit appears nowhere on the page; {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.og_digits) }}%
of those errors are the right digits at the wrong scale. Thinking reduces such errors for {{ H.c3_lower | length }} of
{{ H.c3_models | length }} models; retrying after a failed check raises them for {{ H.c2_raises | length }} of 10.
The findings hold under a second prompt and a repeat run; Markdown tables cut wrong-column errors but expose how
fragile a strict quote check is. We release the questions, every model answer, the code and the protocol.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="intro">1 · Introduction</h2>
<p><a href="{{ V1 }}">Filing-Evidence</a> asked whether small open models can read SEC filings with evidence a machine
can check. They largely can: the best model answered 82.7% of its questions correctly
with a verifiable quote. But a share of answers passed every automatic check and were still wrong: the right line,
the wrong number. Those quiet errors are what make a model unsafe to trust.</p>
<p>v1 suggested where they come from: units printed in a header rather than next to the number, and neighbouring
columns. With 1,000 questions it couldn't test that. This study can. We wrote the hypotheses down first, built
{{ d.n_questions | num }} new questions across ten sectors with difficulty tags computed by code, froze everything,
and only then ran the models. Three research questions, registered in advance:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>RQ1.</b> How does accuracy with checkable evidence vary across ten industry sectors?</li>
  <li><b>RQ2.</b> Which properties of a number make it hard: where its units are stated, how many figures share its line, which column it sits in, its period, and whether it is written in words?</li>
  <li><b>RQ3.</b> Do v1's findings on check-and-retry and thinking hold on a larger and more varied set?</li>
</ul>
<p>The answer to RQ1 is mostly no, and the answer to RQ2 explains why: what makes a number hard is how it is printed,
and that varies within every sector more than between them.</p>

<h2 id="design">2 · Design</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.timeline }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 1.</b> The study in time. The protocol and the frozen questions were committed publicly before any model ran; the robustness checks were fully specified before any of them ran.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>Companies and filings.</b> Ten sectors with ten companies each: banks, insurance, real estate, utilities, oil
and gas, metals and mining, healthcare, technology, media and telecom. These were chosen because their statements are
laid out differently. For each company we took its most recent 10-K and 10-Q, {{ d.n_filings }} filings in all. No
company or datapoint from v1 is reused.</p>
<p><b>Questions and answer key.</b> As in v1, gold answers come from the filers' own inline XBRL tags, and every
question shows the model three pages: the answer page, a neighbouring page and a distractor. There are 1,000 questions
per sector; {{ d.n_answerable | num }} have an answer on the pages shown, and the rest test whether the model says
"not found". The page rendering, wording rules, prompt and checker are v1's, unchanged.</p>
<p><b>Difficulty tags.</b> Every question carries tags computed by code before any run: where the unit is stated
(in the row label, in the line, in a table or column header, in a page header, or nowhere on the page), how many
figures are on the evidence line, whether the answer is the first figure or a later one, the period length, and
whether the amount is written with a word scale ("$12.6 billion").</p>
<p><b>Outcomes.</b> As v1: <i>correct</i> (right value, the quote checks out); <i>confident-wrong</i> (wrong value,
but the quote passes every check: the dangerous case); <i>caught-wrong</i> (wrong, and the check fails);
<i>unverifiable-right</i> (right value, unusable quote); <i>missed</i>; and for absent numbers
<i>abstained correctly</i> or <i>invented</i>.</p>

<h2 id="setup">3 · Models and setups</h2>
<p>The ten models of v1 at the same pinned revisions, plus Qwen3-8B as a supplementary model, all served with vLLM
0.33 on one RTX 4090 capped at 450 W, temperature 0. Three setups: <b>plain</b> (C1) and <b>check + retry</b> (C2) on
all 10,000 questions, and <b>thinking</b> (C3) on a stratified 1,000-question subset for the six models with a
reasoning mode, with up to 8,192 new tokens. In total {{ d.answers_core | num }} answers, including the robustness
checks of section 7.</p>

<h2 id="rq1">4 · Does the sector matter? (RQ1, H1)</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.sector_heatmap }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 2.</b> Accuracy by model and sector, plain setup. Rows differ far more than columns.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Not much. Pooled over the ten main models, accuracy ranges from {{ H.pooled[H.sec_worst].acc | pct }}
({{ H.sec_worst | short | lower }}) to {{ H.pooled[H.sec_best].acc | pct }} ({{ H.sec_best | short | lower }}): a spread of
{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (H.pooled[H.sec_best].acc - H.pooled[H.sec_worst].acc)) }} points, against
{{ "%.0f" | format(100 * (H.acc_top - H.acc_bottom)) }} points between the best and worst model. The order of the models is
much the same in every sector.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.sector_or }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 3.</b> Odds of a correct answer in each sector compared with the easiest, from a logistic regression with a model fixed effect and standard errors clustered by company.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The pre-registered test (H1) is a logistic regression of correctness on sector with a model fixed effect and
standard errors clustered by company: χ²({{ d.h1.df }}) = {{ "%.1f" | format(d.h1.chi2) }},
p = {{ d.h1.p | p }}. <b>H1 is not supported.</b> Companies within a sector differ as much as sectors do. Insurance is
the one sector whose interval excludes the easiest sector on its own (odds ratio
{{ "%.2f" | format(d.h1.odds_ratios_vs_reference["Insurance"].or) }}), but H1 is judged on the omnibus test.</p>
<p><b>The exception that wasn't.</b> Confident-wrong answers vary more by sector than accuracy does, and oil and gas
stands out at {{ H.og.cw_all | pct }} of answerable questions, about twice the rate of telecom or technology. The
reason is specific and checkable: {{ H.og.share_unitless | pct0 }} of its questions have no unit stated anywhere on
the pages shown (the scale appears only on another page of the filing), against 0–8% elsewhere. Those questions are
answered confident-wrong {{ H.og.cw_unitless | pct0 }} of the time. Without them, oil and gas falls to
{{ H.og.cw_without_unitless | pct }}, in line with every other sector. This analysis was not pre-registered and is
reported as exploratory.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.unitless }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 4.</b> Confident-wrong rate by sector, with all items (red) and without items whose unit is stated nowhere on the pages (blue).</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="rq2">5 · What makes a number hard? (RQ2, H2–H6)</h2>
<p>All five pre-registered hypotheses about the tags are supported. Each is a term in one logistic regression on all
tags with a model fixed effect, standard errors clustered by company ({{ hh.n | num }} answers, plain setup, ten main
models), so each effect is estimated holding the others fixed.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.tag_forest }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 5.</b> Odds ratios with 95% intervals for H2–H6. All are significant after Holm correction across H1–H8.</figcaption>
</figure>
<ul>
  <li><b>H2 · Units away from the figure.</b> When the unit is in a table or page header ("in thousands") rather than in the row label, the odds of a confident-wrong answer are {{ "%.1f" | format(hh.H2.or) }} times higher ({{ hh.H2.rates.away | pct }} vs {{ hh.H2.rates.in_the_row_label | pct }}). When the unit is nowhere on the page, the rate reaches {{ hh.H2.rates.none | pct0 }}.</li>
  <li><b>H3 · Crowded lines.</b> Each extra figure on the evidence line lowers the odds of a correct answer by a factor of {{ "%.2f" | format(hh.H3.or) }}: accuracy falls from {{ hh.H3.rates["2"] | pct0 }} on two-figure lines to {{ hh.H3.rates["6"] | pct0 }} on lines with six or more.</li>
  <li><b>H4 · Later columns.</b> When the answer is not the first figure on its line, the odds fall by a factor of {{ "%.2f" | format(hh.H4.or) }} ({{ hh.H4.rates.first | pct0 }} vs {{ hh.H4.rates.later | pct0 }}).</li>
  <li><b>H5 · Year-to-date figures.</b> In 10-Qs, six- and nine-month figures are harder than three-month figures from the same filings (odds ratio {{ "%.2f" | format(hh.H5.or) }}; {{ hh.H5.rates.ytd | pct0 }} vs {{ hh.H5.rates["3_months"] | pct0 }}).</li>
  <li><b>H6 · Word scales.</b> Amounts written as "$12.6 billion" fail differently: mostly as unverifiable-right (the model restates the value in other units, so its quote no longer matches), not as confident-wrong ({{ hh.H6.outcome_mix.word_scale.unverifiable_right | num }} vs {{ hh.H6.outcome_mix.word_scale.confident_wrong | num }}; odds ratio {{ "%.1f" | format(hh.H6.or) }}).</li>
</ul>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.tag_rates }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 6.</b> Raw accuracy by where the number sits. The tests use the regression above; these rates show the size of the effects.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p><b>The mechanism: scale guessing.</b> Of the confident-wrong answers on numbers whose unit is stated nowhere on the
page, {{ "%.0f" | format(100 * H.og_digits) }}% have exactly the right digits with the wrong scale: "52,611 in
thousands" where the filing means millions. The quote passes because the digits match. A checker that compares
digits cannot catch this; only knowing the unit can.</p>

<h2 id="rq3">6 · Do v1's findings hold? (RQ3, H7)</h2>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.h7_arrows }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 7.</b> Confident-wrong answers per 1,000 in the plain setup and after check + retry (all questions) or thinking (the 1,000-question subset).</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>Yes. <b>Thinking lowers confident-wrong answers</b> significantly for {{ H.c3_lower | length }} of the
{{ H.c3_models | length }} main models with a reasoning mode (paired exact McNemar, Holm-corrected within model);
pooled, {{ H.pooled_c3.b10 }} answers stop being confident-wrong and {{ H.pooled_c3.b01 }} start. <b>Check + retry
does not</b>: it never lowers them, and raises them significantly for {{ H.c2_raises | length }} of 10 models, while
raising accuracy for {{ H.c2_acc_up | length }}. Told that its quote failed, a model often finds another line
containing its wrong number. <b>H7 is supported.</b></p>
<p>One exception needs stating plainly. Qwen3.5-9B's accuracy fell with thinking
({{ d.h7.models["qwen3.5-9b"].tests.C3_accuracy.a }} → {{ d.h7.models["qwen3.5-9b"].tests.C3_accuracy.b }} of 900)
because {{ d.c3_truncated["qwen3.5-9b"] }} of its 1,000 answers ran into the pre-registered 8,192-token limit and were cut off. Its confident-wrong
answers still fell ({{ d.h7.models["qwen3.5-9b"].tests.C3_confident_wrong.a }} →
{{ d.h7.models["qwen3.5-9b"].tests.C3_confident_wrong.b }}). A larger budget might change its accuracy result; we
report the limit as fixed.</p>

<h2 id="robust">7 · How robust is this?</h2>
<p>Three checks, fixed in the protocol and specified in full before any of them ran, test whether the findings depend
on how we asked rather than what we asked. All run in the plain setup on the 1,000-question subset.</p>
<p><b>R3 · A repeat run.</b> Running the identical configuration again changes the outcome of
{{ H.r3_min | pct }}–{{ H.r3_max | pct }} of questions for the three models tested, with no significant difference in
accuracy or confident-wrong. Single runs are stable.</p>
<p><b>R2 · A different prompt.</b> A second prompt with different wording and a different worked example (a 10-Q
with quarter and year-to-date columns) leaves the ranking of models almost unchanged (Kendall's
τ = {{ "%.2f" | format(H.tau_r2) }}). Most models move by a few points. The two weakest models are the exception:
Nemotron-Nano-9B-v2 and OLMo-3-7B lose accuracy and gain confident-wrong answers under the new wording.</p>
<p><b>R1 · Markdown tables (H8).</b> The same pages, with tables shown as Markdown: one row per line, column headers
first. Wrong-period-or-column errors fall from {{ H.h8.c1 }} to {{ H.h8.r1 }} (pooled exact McNemar,
p = {{ H.h8.p | p }}). <b>H8 is supported.</b> Confident-wrong answers fall for every model. But strict accuracy
collapses for most models, because many quote the row without its pipes ("Total deposits 239,486" instead of
"| Total deposits | 239,486 |") and the pre-registered check, applied to the page text exactly as shown, rejects
the quote. Under that check the model ranking scrambles (τ = {{ "%.2f" | format(H.tau_r1) }}).</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.r1_bars }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 8.</b> Accuracy in the plain setup, with Markdown tables under the strict quote check, and with Markdown tables when the pipes are ignored (exploratory).</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>To separate quoting from reading we graded R1 a second time with the pipes ignored, a decision made after seeing
the strict results and therefore exploratory. Most of the collapse disappears, and the ranking returns
(τ = {{ "%.2f" | format(H.tau_pf) }}). The best models read Markdown tables as well as flat text; the others lose up to
{{ "%.0f" | format(H.r1_pf_max_loss) }} points ({{ H.r1_pf_max_loss_model | name }}). Both gradings tell the same practical story: layout changes which errors a model makes, and an
evidence check must be robust to it.</p>
<figure>
  <div class="canvas">{{ F.rank_slope }}</div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure 9.</b> Model ranks by accuracy under each check.</figcaption>
</figure>
<p>The tag effects of H2–H4 keep their direction under R1 and R2. Together with the stable repeat run, the headline
findings are <b>robust</b> in the sense the protocol defines.</p>

<h2 id="discussion">8 · Discussion</h2>
<p><b>Look at the line, not the industry.</b> Anyone deploying a model to read filings should worry less about the
sector and more about how each number is printed. Numbers whose unit sits in a header, crowded lines, prior-period
columns and year-to-date figures are where errors concentrate, in every sector.</p>
<p><b>Give the model the unit.</b> The most dangerous single case is a number whose unit isn't on the page at all. The
model guesses, and a digit-based check passes the guess. A reader system should carry the document's scale statement
alongside every page it shows, or have the checker verify the scale separately.</p>
<p><b>Think, don't retry.</b> Thinking reduces quiet errors; retrying after a failed check increases them. If budget
allows one intervention, it should be thinking, with a token limit large enough not to cut answers off.</p>
<p><b>Make the evidence check layout-tolerant.</b> A strict character-for-character check is easy to explain but
punishes models for formatting, not reading. Comparing the words and figures of a quote, not its punctuation, keeps
the guarantee and removes the artefact.</p>

<h2 id="limits">9 · Limitations</h2>
<ul>
  <li><b>Associations, not interventions.</b> The tags describe real filings; we did not move a unit from a row label to a header and re-ask. The regression holds the other tags fixed and R1 is a true intervention on layout, but the tag effects are observational.</li>
  <li><b>One hardware and serving configuration.</b> One RTX 4090 at 450 W, vLLM 0.33, temperature 0.</li>
  <li><b>A fixed thinking budget.</b> 8,192 new tokens cut off a fifth of Qwen3.5-9B's thinking answers.</li>
  <li><b>R1's rendering is imperfect.</b> SEC tables are irregular; in {{ d.r1_header_aligned | pct0 }} of tables with a header, the header row has as many cells as the first data row. The rest are misaligned to some degree.</li>
  <li><b>Exploratory parts are labelled.</b> The oil-and-gas analysis and the pipe-insensitive R1 grading were not pre-registered as tests.</li>
  <li><b>US filings only,</b> in English, from companies large enough to file inline XBRL.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="repro">10 · Reproducibility</h2>
<p>Everything is public: the protocol (every change after it was finalised is logged in its §14, with the date and whether any result existed at the time), the code, the frozen questions (fingerprint
<code>9a007d80</code>), the Markdown pages of R1 (fingerprint <code>7cdf066e</code>) and every model reply with its
reasoning. In a fresh clone, <code>python hf_data.py pull</code> downloads the data and verifies both fingerprints;
re-grading every stored reply reproduces every <code>summary.json</code> byte for byte, and
<code>analyze.py</code> reproduces every statistic in this report. The pre-registration commits are public in
<a href="https://github.com/pramana-research/filing-evidence/commit/00260c43e78ad0f53fcc23ddec6645b97c2559b7">pramana-research/filing-evidence</a>.</p>

